# Workout Live Preview System — Client Implementation Plan

## Goal

Build a near-live workout video streaming system using browser-native recording.

The system does NOT use:
- MediaMTX
- WebRTC streaming
- HLS
- Server-side transcoding

Instead:
- Browser captures webcam
- Browser encodes video into WebM chunks
- Chunks are uploaded periodically to object storage (Cloudflare R2)
- Viewers fetch and play chunks using Media Source Extensions (MSE)

Target experience:
- Maximum ~2 minute delay
- Up to 9 simultaneous preview tiles
- Low infrastructure cost
- CDN-friendly playback


==================================================
1. Broadcaster Client (Recording Side)
==================================================

Flow:

Webcam
  ↓
getUserMedia()
  ↓
MediaRecorder API
  ↓
120-second WebM chunks
  ↓
Upload chunk to backend/R2


Implementation:

Use:

navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia()

Example constraints:

{
  video: {
    width: 640,
    height: 360,
    frameRate: 15
  },
  audio: false
}


Target output:

360p
15 FPS
~300-400 kbps


MediaRecorder:

Create chunks every 120 seconds.

Example:

Room:
abc123

Generated files:

abc123/
  chunk_000001.webm
  chunk_000002.webm
  chunk_000003.webm


Each chunk:
- Is immutable
- Is uploaded once
- Never modified


Upload flow:

1. Start MediaRecorder
2. Receive Blob from dataavailable event
3. Upload Blob
4. Continue recording


The upload process must handle:
- Retry on failure
- Chunk ordering
- Network interruptions
- Resuming after reconnect


==================================================
2. Viewer Client (Playback Side)
==================================================

Flow:

R2/CDN
  ↓
Download WebM chunks
  ↓
Media Source Extensions
  ↓
HTML video element


The viewer should NOT download complete videos.

The player maintains a rolling buffer.


Example:

Available:

chunk_100
chunk_101
chunk_102


Player plays:

chunk_101

while preparing:

chunk_102


==================================================
3. Playback Algorithm
==================================================

Each tile has its own player instance.

For each room:

Maintain:

- Current playback chunk
- Latest available chunk
- Buffer duration


Target delay:

~120 seconds behind live edge


Algorithm:

1. Find latest uploaded chunk
2. Start playback from:
   
   latest chunk - 1 chunk


3. Append chunks sequentially using MSE:

fetch(chunk.webm)

↓

arrayBuffer()

↓

sourceBuffer.appendBuffer()


4. Continue polling for new chunks


Example:

Latest:

chunk_500


Playback:

chunk_499


When chunk_500 becomes available:

append after chunk_499


==================================================
4. Multi Tile Preview
==================================================

Maximum:

9 simultaneous video tiles


Each tile has:

- Independent MediaSource
- Independent buffer
- Independent chunk polling


Optimization:

Only play visible tiles.

Use:

IntersectionObserver


Rules:

Visible tile:
- Fetch chunks
- Decode
- Play


Hidden tile:
- Pause
- Release buffers


==================================================
5. Error Handling
==================================================

Network delay:

Do NOT immediately fail playback.


If next chunk is unavailable:

1. Continue displaying current buffered content
2. Retry fetching
3. Resume when chunk arrives


The system prioritizes:

Smooth playback > minimum latency


Possible behaviors:

- Hold last frame
- Wait for next chunk
- Dynamically increase delay


==================================================
6. Chunk Discovery
==================================================

Initial MVP:

Use predictable chunk naming.

Example:

roomId/

chunk_000001.webm
chunk_000002.webm


Viewer can determine:

Current latest chunk number

through backend API.


Example:

GET /rooms/{roomId}/latest


Response:

{
  "latestChunk": 502
}


The player then requests:

chunk_501.webm
chunk_502.webm


==================================================
7. Storage/CDN Assumptions
==================================================

Files are immutable.

Important:

Do NOT overwrite existing chunks.


Good:

/room123/chunk_001.webm

Bad:

/room123/latest.webm


Reason:

Immutable objects have excellent CDN cache behavior.


